from common import lesson, lab, cite, out

LESSONS = [
lesson(8,2,'orthogonality-and-removing-a-direction','Orthogonality, Gram–Schmidt, and removing a direction',
 'To take a direction out of every activation you subtract its projection. Doing that for several directions in turn is Gram–Schmidt, and what remains is not what you might hope.',
 '''<h2 id="orthogonal">Orthogonal and orthonormal</h2>
<p>Two vectors are <strong>orthogonal</strong> when their dot product is zero. A set is <strong>orthonormal</strong> when every vector has norm 1 and every pair is orthogonal. Orthonormal sets are the easy case for everything in this course: coordinates are dot products (lesson 06), projections onto different members do not interfere, and the projection onto the subspace they span is the sum of the projections onto each member. For a general set of directions none of that holds; project onto d₁ and then onto d₂, and if d₁ · d₂ ≠ 0 the second projection partly undoes the first.</p>
<h2 id="gram-schmidt">Gram–Schmidt</h2>
<p><strong>Gram–Schmidt</strong> turns any list of vectors into an orthonormal list with the same span. Take the first vector and normalise it. Take the second, subtract its projection onto the first, normalise what is left. Take the third, subtract its projections onto the first two, normalise. If a vector is a combination of the earlier ones, subtracting the projections leaves (up to rounding) zero, and it is dropped: the procedure also discovers dependence. The lab runs it on three directions where the third is deliberately a combination of the first two.</p>
<div class="math">q₁ = v₁ / ‖v₁‖<br>w₂ = v₂ − (v₂ · q₁) q₁,  q₂ = w₂ / ‖w₂‖<br>wₖ = vₖ − Σⱼ&lt;ₖ (vₖ · qⱼ) qⱼ,  qₖ = wₖ / ‖wₖ‖  (drop if ‖wₖ‖ ≈ 0)</div>
<p>In floating point a single pass leaves small non-orthogonality; the reference implementation subtracts the projections twice, which is a standard remedy. The test checks Q Qᵀ = I and that the original vectors are reproduced from their coordinates on Q.</p>
<h2 id="removing">Removing a direction from activations</h2>
<p>To remove direction d from a vector x, subtract the projection: x − (x · d̂) d̂. As a matrix, that is x (I − d̂ d̂ᵀ), the second of the three maps from lesson 04. Applied to every row of X at once, <code>X − np.outer(X @ d̂, d̂)</code>, it produces an activation matrix in which every token has exactly zero component along d. This operation appears in the literature under several names (projecting out, ablating a direction, nulling), and its geometry is always this one line. What it does is precise; what it <em>means</em> for the model is not, and the lab is built to show the gap.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py remove --label is_digit</code></pre>
'''+out('''direction d from the 211 training tokens; before: held-out correlation of projection on d with is_digit: 0.935
after removing d from every token: max |projection on d| = 2.13e-14; norms shrink from median 85.00 to 83.88
refit the mean-difference direction on the edited training tokens: |d2| = 1.89e-14 (was 36.45); the class means now coincide
ridge least-squares probe refitted on the edited activations: held-out correlation -0.053  (compare the probe on the unedited activations: 0.986)
gram_schmidt on [d, d_after_period, d + 0.5 d_after_period]: kept 2 orthonormal vectors; Q @ Q.T =
[[1. 0.]
 [0. 1.]]''')+'''
<p>Line by line. The mean-difference digit direction, built on 211 training tokens, has held-out correlation 0.935 with the digit label before anything is removed. After removal, every token’s component along d is zero to fourteen decimal places, and the median norm has dropped only from 85.00 to 83.88: the direction carried a small fraction of each vector’s length, as lesson 03 found for token 12. Refitting the mean-difference direction on the edited activations gives a vector of norm 10⁻¹⁴, which is an identity rather than a discovery: the difference of class means was d, and d has been removed, so the difference of class means is now zero. The fourth line is the real test. A least-squares probe (lesson 09) fitted on the edited activations reaches held-out correlation −0.053, where on the unedited activations it reached 0.986. On this fixture, for this label, removing the mean-difference direction did remove the linearly readable digit signal, as judged on tokens the probe never saw.</p>
<p>That result is not guaranteed. Run the same command with <code>--label after_period</code> and the refitted probe keeps a held-out correlation of 0.112 against 0.740 before: most of the signal gone, not all. And lesson 15 shows the opposite kind of case, where removing the least-squares direction itself leaves a refitted probe at 0.696. Removing one direction removes one direction. Whether a label stays readable from what is left is a question about the data, answered by refitting on held-out tokens, never by the identity that the removed direction now reads zero.</p>
<h2 id="pythagoras">What removal preserves</h2>
<p>Because the removed part and the remainder are orthogonal, ‖x‖² = (x · d̂)² + ‖x − (x · d̂) d̂‖². The energy accounting is exact: the squared norm lost from each token is exactly its squared coefficient along d. Summed over tokens and divided by n − 1, that is the variance along d, which is the quantity PCA ranks directions by in lesson 11. Removing the top principal component removes more squared norm than removing any other single direction; removing a probe direction removes however much variance happens to lie along it, which can be very little even when the direction predicts the label well.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>“We ablated the feature direction and the behaviour persisted” is interpretable only with the numbers above in hand: what fraction of the norm the direction carried, whether a refitted probe on held-out data still reads the label, and whether the direction removed was the one the behaviour used. A direction that reads a label is not thereby the direction the model computes with.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Removing several non-orthogonal directions one after another and assuming all of them are gone. Removing d₂ after d₁ reintroduces a component along d₁ if d₁ · d₂ ≠ 0. Orthonormalise the set with Gram–Schmidt first, then remove the whole subspace.</p></div>''',
 'After removing d, the mean-difference direction refitted on the same tokens is zero. A colleague concludes “the digit feature has been erased.” Which line of the output supports a weaker but defensible version, and what is it?',
 'The fourth line: a least-squares probe refitted on the edited activations has held-out correlation −0.053. The defensible statement is “after removing d, no linear probe fitted on these 211 tokens reads the digit label on the other 90”, which is a claim about linear readability on this layer and sample. It says nothing about whether the model still computes with digit information through non-linear paths, through other layers, or through directions a bigger sample would reveal, and the after_period case shows a residual signal can survive.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py remove --label after_period'],
 'Remove the after-period direction and read the refitted probe’s held-out correlation against the unedited one.',
 'Expect 0.112 after against 0.740 before. The mean-difference direction captured most but not all of what a probe can read; the remainder lives in directions the mean difference does not point along. Only the held-out refit can tell you that.'), ['3b1b','np-lstsq']),

lesson(9,2,'least-squares-and-linear-probes','Least squares: fitting a direction to labels',
 'A linear probe is a direction chosen so that projections match a label as closely as possible in the squared-error sense. The normal equations say the mismatch is orthogonal to the data.',
 '''<h2 id="problem">The problem</h2>
<p>You have an activation matrix X of shape (n, d) and a label y of length n, one number per token. You want a direction w and an offset b such that X w + b is as close to y as possible. <strong>Least squares</strong> defines “close” as the sum of squared differences, Σᵢ (xᵢ · w + b − yᵢ)², and asks for the w and b that minimise it. The solution is a <strong>linear probe</strong>; its projection x · w + b is the probe’s score for a token. For a binary label the score is a number that should be near 1 on positives and near 0 on negatives.</p>
<h2 id="geometry">The geometry: residual orthogonal to the data</h2>
<p>Centre X and y so that the offset disappears, and look at the columns of X as d vectors in ℝⁿ, one per dimension, each listing that dimension’s value across the n tokens. The prediction X w is a linear combination of those columns, so it lies in their span, a subspace of ℝⁿ. The closest point in a subspace to y is the orthogonal projection of y onto it, by the same picture as lesson 03 with the roles swapped: the subspace is the thing projected onto, y is the thing projected. The residual y − X w is therefore orthogonal to every column of X, which written out is the <strong>normal equations</strong>.</p>
<div class="math">minimise ‖X w − y‖²<br>Xᵀ (y − X w) = 0,  i.e.  XᵀX w = Xᵀ y   (normal equations)<br>with ridge λ: (XᵀX + λI) w = Xᵀ y</div>
<p>XᵀX is the (d, d) matrix of dot products between columns, the uncentred cousin of the covariance matrix of lesson 10, and Xᵀy lists the dot product of each column with the label. In code, <code>numpy.linalg.lstsq</code> solves the problem directly and, when there are more columns than rows so that many w fit exactly, returns the one with the smallest norm. '''+cite('np-lstsq')+''' The exercise asks you to centre, call lstsq, and recover the offset as ȳ − x̄ · w.</p>
<h2 id="fixture">On the fixture</h2>
<p>With 768 dimensions and only 211 training tokens, there are more unknowns than equations. Any label can be fitted exactly, so the training fit is meaningless; what matters is the held-out part. The lab splits the 301 tokens 70/30 with a fixed seed, fits on the training part, and reports both.</p>
<pre class="term"><code>python3 labs/linalg_lab.py probe --label is_digit</code></pre>
'''+out('''label is_digit: 45 ones of 301; train 211 tokens, held out 90; ridge 0.0
least-squares direction w: shape (768,), |w| = 0.0374, intercept b = 0.034
train: correlation(pred, y) = 1.000; mean squared error 0.0000
held out: correlation(pred, y) = 0.986; mean squared error 0.0045
normal equations check on train: max |Xc.T @ residual| = 6.43e-13 (residual is orthogonal to every column)
cos(w, mean-difference direction) = 0.734; held-out correlation using the mean-difference direction instead: 0.935''')+'''
<p>The training correlation is exactly 1.000 with zero error: interpolation, as predicted. The held-out correlation is 0.986, which is the number that means something, and the mean squared error of 0.0045 says the held-out scores are within about 0.07 of the 0/1 label on average. The normal-equations check is the geometry made numerical: the residual’s dot product with every centred column is zero to twelve decimal places. The final line compares the least-squares direction with the mean-difference direction of lesson 03: their cosine is 0.734, so they are related but different, and on held-out tokens the probe does better (0.986 against 0.935). Least squares uses the covariance between dimensions to cancel what the classes share; the mean difference does not.</p>
<h2 id="ridge">Ridge: a penalty on the norm</h2>
<p>When w is not unique, or when the data have directions of tiny variance that lstsq will exploit, add λ‖w‖² to the objective. The normal equations gain λ on the diagonal, the solution becomes unique for any λ &gt; 0, and the direction shrinks toward zero as λ grows. The practice command below uses λ = 100 on a harder label. Choosing λ is a model-selection question whose only honest answer is held-out performance; the <a href="../../../statistics/statistics-tut/index.html">statistics course</a> covers the logic.</p>
<div class="evals"><span class="label">In interpretability work</span><p>Linear probes are the standard first test of “is X linearly represented at this layer”, and ARENA’s probing section trains them on a model playing a game. '''+cite('arena')+''' The two numbers to demand from any probe are the held-out score and the baseline for that score (lesson 14). The training score of an overparameterised probe is 1 for any label, including random ones.</p></div>
<div class="pitfall"><span class="label">Pitfall</span><p>Treating the probe direction w as “the feature direction the model uses”. Least squares finds the direction that best predicts the label under squared error on this sample; the model may compute with a different direction that correlates with it, and lesson 08 showed that removing w does not necessarily erase the label.</p></div>''',
 'The probe fits the 211 training tokens with correlation exactly 1.000. If you shuffled the digit labels among the training tokens and refitted, what training correlation would you get, and what held-out correlation would you expect?',
 'Training correlation 1.000 again: with 768 unknowns and 211 equations, lstsq fits any labels exactly, including shuffled ones. Held-out correlation near zero, because there is no relation between the shuffled labels and the activations to carry over. The pair (1.000 train, ≈0 held out) is the signature of an interpolating fit of noise, and it is why lesson 14 reports the permutation baseline for the held-out score and ignores the training score.',
 lab(['python3 labs/linalg_lab.py probe --label capitalised --ridge 100'],
 'Fit a ridge probe for “first character is upper case” with λ = 100. Compare train and held-out correlations with the digit probe.',
 'The training correlation will be below 1 now (ridge stops exact interpolation) and the held-out correlation well below the digit probe’s 0.986, somewhere around 0.68 in the transcript. Capitalisation after block 6 is less linearly readable on 211 tokens than digit-ness, or needs more data; the probe cannot say which. The mean squared error line tells you how far scores sit from the labels on average.'), ['np-lstsq','arena','3b1b']),
]
